---
/**
 * The sign-up cluster on a schedule row — one quiet link per platform the club actually signs people up on.
 *
 * Why this is a component and not a paragraph inside `EventRow.astro` (S019, D272, 2026-11-10). The operator's
 * ruling is that the site does not hold RSVPs: "the website just needs functionality to make it easy for a
 * user to rsvp at a site that they prefer to rsvp on". So the affordance is a set of links sitting on the row
 * that already prints the date, and the only place a row's set of links can come from is `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`.
 * A component is what makes that import unavoidable: a second copy of the link rule inside the row markup is
 * the same defect class as a second copy inside a gate (D238, D264), and `check-rsvp-page.mjs` compares the
 * painted bytes of this component against `signUpPaths()` output — which is only a meaningful assertion if
 * the markup is generated from that function.
 *
 * The grammar is DESIGN.md's **quiet label**, not a control (D139, D140): `text-label font-medium text-ink`,
 * underline at `decoration-rule`, `min-h-8` (32px), focus ring, **no fill, no keyline, no `data-control`**.
 * A row is one boundary (its own `inset-shadow-rule`, D220). Putting a 48px filled control inside it would
 * draw a second box inside the row's ring — the exact defect `check:render` fails on for notices (D149-3c,
 * design/records/0018) — and four 48px buttons under every date would out-shout the date, which the band is
 * designed to make the loudest thing on the page.
 *
 * Order is not decorative: Meetup first (the club's calendar of record for sign-up, D259), then Discord, then
 * Facebook. A visitor who keeps opening one platform gets that platform first from then on (S022, D281) — but
 * the reorder happens in the browser, in `SignUpLinksCluster.svelte`, and the bytes this file builds are still
 * the canonical order, so the gate's "what the resolver says" comparison is against the server's answer, not
 * against one visitor's history. A platform with no **verified** URL is not in the list at all — it is not rendered, and
 * `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` asserts the absence from the built bytes rather than trusting this file's
 * silence (S019's open question 3). The same gate asserts the mirror, in the other direction (D280): every
 * platform `src/config.json` declares must appear in **every** printed cluster, so a component that quietly
 * drops one is caught as surely as one that invents a platform.
 *
 * The labels name the platform and the action (D273, 2026-11-11). The operator's ruling was that a visitor
 * should see several ways in, plainly, rather than one button called "Details and sign-up" that happens to go
 * to Meetup: "I want to present many options of platforms on which a visitor can use to RSVP... friendly,
 * informational, open". So each label says which site it opens and what the visitor does there — *Sign up on
 * Meetup*, *Join the Discord chat*, *Join the Facebook group*. A label that names the platform needs no
 * hidden " on Meetup" suffix; a label that is an event title (the `several` state) carries it in the
 * screen-reader text instead, so two lifted-out links are never both called "Proofs".
 *
 * Facebook is the one platform this rig cannot measure. Measured 2026-11-11 (Linux native, curl, browser UA,
 * redirects followed): Facebook answers **400 to every anonymous GET** from here — the club's group
 * (`/groups/matheapolis`), a group that does not exist, and a page alike — so a status code is not evidence
 * for or against any particular URL, and a gate that treated one as such would be checking nothing
 * (D280). The club's group is public and the operator named it (`.notes/SESSIONS.md`, 2026-11-11), so the
 * gate holds that key on the two things it *can* read: `FACEBOOK_GROUP_URL_SHAPE` in `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`, and a
 * dated, named confirmation (`_verified.facebookGroup.confirmedBy` / `confirmedAt`) inside the same 60-day
 * window the probe stamps use. When a probe that can reach Facebook exists, its 2xx/3xx stamp replaces the
 * confirmation; nothing in this markup changes, because the component only ever reads `paths.destinations`.
 *
 * Discord is not just a chat. A guild scheduled event has its own page, `discord.com/events/<guild>/<id>/`,
 * and that is a sign-up surface (D274). Whether one exists for a printed date is not guessed: it is read from
 * the guild's own endpoint into `data/discord-events.json` and matched on the same date + wall-clock hour as
 * a Meetup page. Where the guild has no scheduled event for a date, the Discord link is the invite, labelled
 * as the chat — the site never tells a visitor to RSVP on a platform that has no page for that date.
 * Guild scheduled events are `GuildOnly`, so a non-member landing on one is asked to join the server first;
 * that is why the label is "Sign up on Discord" and not a promise of a one-click attend.
 *
 * The date, the hour and the sign-up pages all come from committed snapshots (`data/events.json`,
 * `data/meetup-events.json`, `data/discord-events.json`) — the build never reaches the network (D73's
 * topology). Both event snapshots carry headcounts upstream; `meetupView()` and `discordView()` in
 * `src/lib/rsvp.mjs` are whitelist projections that drop them, so no count can reach this markup (D272).
 * `check:content` and `check-rsvp-page.mjs` are the two ends of that rule: one stops a count entering the
 * render path, the other reads the painted page and fails if one got there.
 *
 * `data-rsvp-platform`, `data-rsvp-date`, `data-rsvp-hour`, `data-rsvp-state`, `data-rsvp-snapshot`,
 * `data-rsvp-age`, `data-rsvp-discord-snapshot` and `data-rsvp-discord-age` are probe handles for
 * `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs`, not styling. `data-rsvp-hour` is what lets the gate re-run the resolver from
 * the painted page alone: the row's own hour is painted as prose ("time not set" when the organizer has not
 * settled it, D169), and a gate that re-parses prose breaks the day the prose is rewritten (D173).
 * They are written per link rather than derived from the row's
 * `data-date` because the homepage's compact card and the `/schedule` list are different containers (D227:
 * one row shape, two surfaces), and a gate that has to know which container it is inside is a gate that
 * breaks when the layout moves (D173).
 */
import config from '../config.json';
import meetupSnapshot from '../../data/meetup-events.json';
import discordSnapshot from '../../data/discord-events.json';
import { signUpPaths, snapshotAgeDays } from '../lib/rsvp.mjs';
import SignUpLinksCluster from '../components/SignUpLinksCluster.svelte';
import PlatformMarks from './PlatformMarks.astro';
import { spriteFor } from '../lib/platform-marks.mjs';
import type { UpcomingItem } from '../lib/content.ts';

type Props = {
  item: UpcomingItem;
};

const { item } = Astro.props;
const paths = signUpPaths(item, meetupSnapshot.events, config.URLs, discordSnapshot.events);
// The ages the page gate will see. Stated here so a reader of the built HTML can tell *why* a date has no
// page: `data-rsvp-snapshot` is the stamp, and `check:rsvp` is the gate that fails a build over it.
const age = snapshotAgeDays(meetupSnapshot);
const discordAge = snapshotAgeDays(discordSnapshot);
---

<!-- The cluster itself lives in `src/components/SignUpLinksCluster.svelte` (S022, D281). This file only
  decides *what* the row signs people up on; the component decides *in what order* a particular visitor sees
  it. Astro server-renders the island, so the bytes `check:rsvp-page` reads are produced here at build time in
  the canonical order (Meetup, Discord, Facebook) — the visitor's remembered platform is applied in the
  browser, never on the server, so a JavaScript-off visitor, a crawler and a screen reader all get the same
  order the gate asserts. Everything passed below is a primitive or a plain array because it is serialised
  into the island tag: the snapshots and the config never leave the server. `client:visible` is the same
  strategy the /chat page uses for `DiscordSummary.svelte`. -->
{spriteFor(Astro.locals) && <PlatformMarks />}
<SignUpLinksCluster
  client:visible
  destinations={paths.destinations}
  note={paths.note}
  date={paths.date}
  signupState={paths.state}
  hour={paths.hour ?? 'unsettled'}
  meetupSnapshot={meetupSnapshot.source.fetchedAt}
  meetupAge={age === null ? 'unknown' : age.toFixed(1)}
  discordSnapshot={discordSnapshot.source?.fetchedAt ?? 'none'}
  discordAge={discordAge === null ? 'unknown' : discordAge.toFixed(1)}
/>
